  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>A comms room inspection is a physical walk-round: power, temperature, cabling, access and housekeeping</li><li>The common killers are dead UPS batteries, blocked cooling and stored combustibles, all catchable on a walk</li><li>Record each item at the rack with a photo and a reading, not from memory afterwards</li><li>A photo trail and timestamp turn the check into evidence for insurance and audits</li><li>Quickler captures the physical check over WhatsApp; it is not a network or logical audit tool</li></ul></div><h2>Why a comms room needs a regular physical check</h2><p>A comms room is often a cupboard with a rack in it, and also the single point of failure for a whole office or site. The failures that take it down are slow: a UPS past its battery life, a clogged cooling filter, a temperature creeping up, cabling that hides a fault. A short, repeatable physical inspection on a schedule catches these while they are cheap to fix, instead of the day of an outage.</p><h2>The comms room inspection checklist</h2><h3>1. Power and UPS</h3><p>Confirm the UPS is online and not on bypass. Note the load percentage and battery age against its rated life (typically three to five years). Check the last self-test date and result. Look for tripped or unlabelled breakers, and for kit fed through domestic extension leads. Photograph the UPS display so the reading is on record. A UPS with tired batteries reports healthy until the first real cut, so the battery age and test date are the numbers that matter.</p><h3>2. Temperature and cooling</h3><p>Read the room temperature against the equipment's threshold, commonly kept in the low twenties Celsius. Confirm the aircon or ventilation is running and not in fault. Check filters are clean and vents are not blocked by stored kit. Feel for hot spots behind the rack. A blocked filter or failed fan just lets the temperature drift, ageing hardware quietly, so a logged monthly reading is worth the minute it takes.</p><h3>3. Cabling</h3><p>Dressed and labelled patch leads are not vanity. A tangle hides faults, blocks airflow and makes every change risky. Note untidy runs and unlabelled cables for tidying and identification.</p><h3>4. Housekeeping</h3><p>Check the floor is clear, no combustible materials or cardboard are stored in the room, and there is no sign of water ingress or heavy dust.</p><h3>5. Physical access</h3><p>Confirm the door locks, the access list is current, and there is a visitor log if the room holds anything sensitive. A room anyone can walk into is a physical-security finding in its own right.</p><h2>Record it as you walk, not afterwards</h2><p>The point of a checklist is that nothing gets skipped and nothing gets remembered wrong. Snap the UPS display, voice-note the temperature reading, photograph the cable run you want fixed. The record builds while you are in the room, so the write-up is done before you leave. A record with a photo and a timestamp against each item is evidence, not just a note.</p><h2>What Quickler does and does not do here</h2><p>Quickler suits the physical walk-round: capturing the readings, photos and findings as you move round the rack, and producing a clean timestamped record. That record is useful evidence for insurers, landlords and internal audit. It does not test the network, read switch configuration or check logical security. It captures what a competent engineer observes physically. If your check is really a network audit, quickler is not the tool for that half.</p><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> the check is only as good as the engineer following it. Quickler makes the physical evidence easy to gather; it does not decide whether the room passes. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if you want to see the workflow.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What should a comms room inspection checklist include?</h3><p>A comms room inspection should cover power (UPS status, load, battery age, last self-test), temperature and cooling (room reading against threshold, aircon running, filters clean), cabling (dressed, labelled, airflow clear), housekeeping (floor clear, no combustibles, no water ingress), and physical access (door locks, access list current). Record each item with a reading or photo as you walk.</p><h3>How often should a server room be inspected?</h3><p>A physical comms or server room inspection is commonly done monthly or quarterly, depending on how critical the room is and what your insurer or ISO 27001 controls require. Environmental readings like temperature benefit from more frequent or continuous checks, while a full physical walk-round monthly or quarterly catches the slower problems.</p><h3>Can I run a server room check over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's inspection workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The engineer receives each checklist item in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with a reading, a voice note or a photo, and the completed record generates automatically. No app to install and no login. It captures the physical check, not the network audit.</p><h3>Does a comms room inspection count towards ISO 27001?</h3><p>A physical comms room inspection provides evidence for several ISO 27001:2022 Annex A physical controls, such as secure areas, physical entry and equipment siting and protection. It is one input to an ISO 27001 audit, not the whole thing, and the certification decision rests with a qualified auditor.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/data-centre-audit-software-uk.html\">Data centre audit software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/iso-27001-physical-security-audit-uk.html\">ISO 27001 physical security audit</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/dr-site-audit-checklist-uk.html\">DR site audit checklist</a></li></ul>",
